WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.193 --> 00:00:02.838
The Olympic Games are a sporting event like no other.

2
00:00:02.840 --> 00:00:11.246
They are the world’s largest sporting eventㅡthe Games have more athletes, more sports and more participant countries than any other sports competition in the world.

3
00:00:11.247 --> 00:00:16.167
In 2004, the Olympic Games are returning to their ancient birthplace in Greece.

4
00:00:16.168 --> 00:00:21.546
The Athens Olympic Games will be held from the 13th to the 29th of August 2004.

5
00:00:21.547 --> 00:00:25.863
The competition schedule includes 28 Olympics sports at 35 venues.

6
00:00:25.864 --> 00:00:32.801
While the ancient Olympics have a long history of over one thousand years, the modern Olympics have a much shorter history.

7
00:00:32.802 --> 00:00:38.906
In 1894, a French aristocrat named Pierre de Coubertin dreamt of reviving them.

8
00:00:38.907 --> 00:00:46.098
Like the Ancient Greeks, he believed sport had an essential role in the education system, and in the formation of strong moral character.

9
00:00:46.099 --> 00:00:52.256
He was also a keen sportsman himself, and practiced the sports of boxing, fencing, horse-riding and rowing.

10
00:00:52.257 --> 00:00:56.776
Coubertin decided the best way to promote sport was to revive the Olympic Games.

11
00:00:56.777 --> 00:01:03.944
He formed an International Olympic Committee (IOC), whose job it was to organize the first modern Olympic Games.

12
00:01:03.945 --> 00:01:09.917
They did this successfully and in 1896, the first modern Olympic Games were held in Athens, Greece.

13
00:01:09.918 --> 00:01:15.722
They were held in Athens as a reminder that the Olympic Games originated in Greece.

14
00:01:15.723 --> 00:01:20.616
The Olympic Games in 1896 featured many references to the ancient Games.

15
00:01:20.617 --> 00:01:25.473
The majority of the competitions took place in the ancient stadium, which was restored for the occasion.

16
00:01:25.474 --> 00:01:31.795
Some of the sports from the ancient Games were echoed in the modern games, including weightlifting, wrestling and athletics.

17
00:01:31.796 --> 00:01:36.239
There was also cycling, fencing, gymnastics, swimming, tennis and shooting.

18
00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:43.056
The organizers even went as far as inventing a new race, inspired by a legendary event in Ancient Greek history: the marathon race.

19
00:01:43.057 --> 00:01:51.731
This race commemorates the exploit of a soldier who, in 490 BC, ran from the town of Marathon to Athens to announce that the Persians had been defeated in battle.

20
00:01:51.732 --> 00:01:57.828
Legend has it that the soldier delivered the message "Niki!" ("victory"), then collapsed and died.The

21
00:01:57.829 --> 00:02:01.281
distance was approximately 42 km. The

22
00:02:01.282 --> 00:02:07.516
modern Olympic Games have twenty-eight different sports. The IOC sets the program and decides which sports will be included. Unlike

23
00:02:07.517 --> 00:02:16.991
the ancient Games, individuals represent their country, and team sports like rowing, softball, baseball, basketball, soccer, volleyball and hockey are included. Until

24
00:02:16.992 --> 00:02:24.223
1992, lesser known sports were also included as "demonstration sports" alongside the official Olympic program. Some

25
00:02:24.224 --> 00:02:30.113
demonstration sports include bandy (soccer on ice), dogsled racing and water skiing. The

26
00:02:30.114 --> 00:02:48.944
Olympic program has come a long way since 1896: some sports have been discontinued (e.g. golf, polo and tug-of-war), others were dropped and then reintroduced (e.g. archery), while several new sports have been added (e.g. the triathlon and taekwondo). For

27
00:02:48.945 --> 00:02:53.635
both Coubertin and the ancient Greeks, the Olympics were not just about sport. He

28
00:02:53.636 --> 00:03:05.457
believed this sports competition was different because it had principles: to strive for perfection, to represent the best athletes in the world and for everyone involved to display high moral character and a spirit of fair play. The

29
00:03:05.458 --> 00:03:10.270
symbols of the Olympic Games reflect the ideals of Coubertin and the Olympic movement. The

30
00:03:10.271 --> 00:03:14.463
most important Olympic symbols are the rings, the motto and the flame. The

31
00:03:14.464 --> 00:03:16.707
rings represent five continents. They

32
00:03:16.708 --> 00:03:20.962
are interlaced to show the meeting of the athletes of the whole world during the Olympic Games. On

33
00:03:20.963 --> 00:03:24.142
the Olympic flag, the rings appear on a white background. The

34
00:03:24.143 --> 00:03:28.853
six colors of the flag, including the white background, represent all nations. The

35
00:03:28.854 --> 00:03:36.469
motto is made up of three Latin words: Citius, Altius, Fortius (Faster, Higher, Stronger). These

36
00:03:36.470 --> 00:03:43.950
three words encourage the athlete to give his or her best during competition, and to view this effort as a victory in itself. In

37
00:03:43.951 --> 00:03:50.792
memory of the Olympic Games’ origins, a flame is lit in Olympia, Greece, some months before the opening of the Games. The

38
00:03:50.793 --> 00:03:53.513
Olympic flame can only be lit by the sun’s rays. The

39
00:03:53.514 --> 00:03:57.963
flame, burning in the Olympic torch, is carried from Olympia to the host country. Past

40
00:03:57.964 --> 00:04:05.355
and present Olympic athletes, as well as ordinary citizens, run with the torch through towns and cities. Modern

41
00:04:05.356 --> 00:04:09.145
Olympians are as legendary as the ancient Greek heroes before them. As

42
00:04:09.146 --> 00:04:16.605
Anita L. DeFrantz, a former Olympian, says, "Excellence comes in every shape, size, race and sex . . . Each

43
00:04:16.606 --> 00:04:21.010
sport takes a special skill and determination for a person to ascend to the top." Soonyung

44
00:04:21.011 --> 00:04:28.961
Kim was only 17 years old when she won gold medals in both the individual and team archery events at the 1988 Seoul Olympics. In

45
00:04:28.962 --> 00:04:35.795
the 1992 Barcelona Olympics, Kim won the silver medal in the individual event and another gold medal in the team event. She

46
00:04:35.796 --> 00:04:38.192
then retired at the age of 21. Over

47
00:04:38.193 --> 00:04:41.103
the next seven years, Kim married and raised two children. In

48
00:04:41.104 --> 00:04:46.785
1999, she started training again and qualified for the South Korean Olympic team for the Sydney Games. In

49
00:04:46.786 --> 00:04:50.193
Sydney, she finished third in the individual archery event. Soonyung

50
00:04:50.194 --> 00:04:55.514
Kim now had a complete set of medalsㅡgold, silver and bronze. One

51
00:04:55.515 --> 00:04:59.179
of Korea’s most determined Olympic heroes was Keechung Sohn. As

52
00:04:59.180 --> 00:05:06.492
Korea was occupied by Japanese forces, Sohn had to participate in the 1936 Berlin Olympics as a Japanese team member. He

53
00:05:06.494 --> 00:05:13.496
was forced to use a Japanese name, Kitei Son. At the Games, Sohn resisted the pressure from the Japanese. He

54
00:05:13.497 --> 00:05:20.222
always signed with his Korean name, and whenever he was asked where he was from, he explained that Korea was a separate country. Sohn

55
00:05:20.223 --> 00:05:25.025
became a Korean national hero when he won the Berlin Olympic marathon by more than two minutes. One

56
00:05:25.026 --> 00:05:32.565
newspaper, Dong-a-Ilbo , published a photo of Sohn on the victory platform-but with one change: they painted over the Japanese flag on his sweatshirt.

57
00:05:32.566 --> 00:05:36.152
Staff members were jailed and the newspaper’s publication was suspended.

58
00:05:36.153 --> 00:05:40.293
Sohn carried the South Korean flag in the Opening Ceremony of the London Olympics.

59
00:05:40.294 --> 00:05:45.118
Forty years later, Keechung Sohn entered the Seoul Olympic Stadium bearing the Olympic torch.

60
00:05:45.119 --> 00:05:51.208
The 76-year-old Sohn ran with pride and the crowd cheered him on.

61
00:05:51.209 --> 00:05:55.883
Like the ancient Olympics, the modern Olympics have been the place for politics as well as sports events.

62
00:05:55.884 --> 00:06:03.953
In 1968 in the Mexico City Olympics, American athletes Tommy Smith and John Carlos demonstrated against racial segregation in the United States.

63
00:06:03.954 --> 00:06:09.293
When the American flag was raised to celebrate their victory, the athletes raised their fists and bowed their heads.

64
00:06:09.294 --> 00:06:12.420
This gesture showed their support for the rights of African Americans.

65
00:06:12.421 --> 00:06:18.437
As a result, however, they were sent home. In 1972, Palestinian terrorists took Israeli athletes hostage.

66
00:06:18.438 --> 00:06:24.672
They wanted to bring the world’s attention to Palestine’s situation, and demand the release of Arab hostages held by the Israelis.

67
00:06:24.673 --> 00:06:30.959
The event ended in tragedy when the hostages were executed by the terrorists, before in turn they were killed by the police.

68
00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:37.630
At the opening ceremony of the 2000 games in Sydney, Australia, South Korea and North Korea marched together under a single flag.

69
00:06:37.631 --> 00:06:41.766
They were greeted by thunderous applause and tears.

70
00:06:41.767 --> 00:06:46.721
When the founder of the modern Olympic Games was asked, "Why did you restore the Olympic Games?"

71
00:06:46.722 --> 00:06:53.234
Pierre de Coubertin replied, "To ennoble and strengthen sports and to ensure their independence and duration . . ."

72
00:06:53.235 --> 00:06:56.281
The 2004 Athens Olympics starts soon.

73
00:06:56.282 --> 00:07:06.148
During this time, athletes and spectators alike will experience some intense moments: a new record, dignity in defeat and victory, and the feeling of being part of another Olympiad.

